Q: Dart timeout on await Future A: <blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>I need it because in some networking operation, the connection is sometimes producing silent error.</p>
</blockquote>
</blockquote>
<p>These <code>silent</code> errors are documented for the <code>http</code> package in the <code>close()</code> method:</p>
<ul>
<li>Resources are always freed</li>
<li>The behavior of asynchronous methods is undefined in open connections</li>
</ul>
<p><code>Resources are always freed</code> – this is good and reliable.<br />
<code>The behavior of asynchronous methods is undefined in open connections</code> – this is not a problem if you address this issue at the application level (without affecting system calls).</p>
<p>Proof:</p>
<p><a href="https://github.com/dart-lang/sdk/blob/main/sdk/lib/_internal/vm/bin/socket_patch.dart" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://github.com/dart-lang/sdk/blob/main/sdk/lib/_internal/vm/bin/socket_patch.dart</a></p>
<pre class="lang-dart prettyprint-override"><code>import 'dart:async';
import 'dart:io';

import 'package:http/http.dart' as http;
import 'package:multitasking/multitasking.dart';

void main(List&lt;String&gt; args) async {
  final demoServer = await HttpServer.bind('localhost', 8080);
  final serverUrl = 'http://${demoServer.address.host}:${demoServer.port}';
  print('Server running on $serverUrl');

  Timer.run(() async {
    await for (final request in demoServer) {
      unawaited(() async {
        final response = request.response;
        try {
          final requestedUri = request.requestedUri;
          final pathSegments = requestedUri.pathSegments;
          if (pathSegments.length == 2 &amp;&amp; pathSegments[0] == 'timeout') {
            final timeout = int.tryParse(pathSegments[1]);
            if (timeout != null) {
              await Future&lt;void&gt;.delayed(Duration(seconds: timeout));
            }
          }

          response.headers.contentType = ContentType.text;
          response.write('Hello from Dart server!');
        } finally {
          unawaited(response.close());
        }
      }());
    }
  });

  _sw = Stopwatch()..start();
  try {
    final cts = CancellationTokenSource();
    final token = cts.token;
    final uri = Uri.parse('$serverUrl/timeout/10');
    Timer(Duration(seconds: 3), cts.cancel);
    _message('Send request: $uri');
    final response = await _doWork(uri, token);
    _message('Response: $response');
  } catch (e) {
    _message('Error $e');
  }

  try {
    final cts = CancellationTokenSource();
    final token = cts.token;
    final uri = Uri.parse('$serverUrl/hello');
    //Timer(Duration(seconds: 3), cts.cancel);
    _message('Send request: $uri');
    final response = await _doWork(uri, token);
    _message('Response: $response');
  } catch (e) {
    _message('Error $e');
  }

  _message('Shut down server');
  await demoServer.close(force: true);
  _message('Done');
}

late final Stopwatch _sw;

Future&lt;String&gt; _doWork(Uri uri, CancellationToken token) async {
  token.throwIfCancelled();
  final client = http.Client();
  final bytes = &lt;int&gt;[];
  await runCancellable(token, client.close, () async {
    try {
      final response = await runAndDetach(() {
        return client.send(http.Request('GET', uri));
      });

      if (response.statusCode != 200) {
        throw StateError('Http error (${response.statusCode}): $uri');
      }

      final iterator = StreamIterator(response.stream);
      await runCancellable(token, iterator.cancel, () async {
        try {
          while (await iterator.moveNext()) {
            bytes.addAll(iterator.current);
          }
        } finally {
          await iterator.cancel();
        }
      });
    } finally {
      client.close();
    }
  });

  token.throwIfCancelled();
  return String.fromCharCodes(bytes);
}

void _message(String text) {
  print('${_sw.elapsedMilliseconds}: $text');
}

</code></pre>
<p>Results:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>Server running on http://localhost:8080
1: Send request: http://localhost:8080/timeout/10
3016: Error TaskCanceledError
3016: Send request: http://localhost:8080/hello
3051: Response: Hello from Dart server!
3051: Shut down server
3056: Done
Exited.
</code></pre>

Q: Redirect old URLS with SEO to new app. Angular + Symfony A: <p>I came to conclusion that it was wrong approach... I thought of Symfony as simply an API, not a tool that I can use to generate a redirection map. I created a map for nginx with redirections from old URL to new and served it to nginx.</p>
<pre><code>Old_url_n new_url_n;
</code></pre>
<p>When you have a map, to serve it to your vhost you have to add this to your vhost file:</p>
<pre><code>    # Redirections
    location /your_url/ {

        if ($redirect_target) {
            return 301 $redirect_target;
        }

        return 301 /home;
    }
</code></pre>
<p>What it does is if an url after your_website/your_path/ exists in map file  (like localhost/details/123), it will properly redirect to it's destination with 301 code (moved permamently). If not, it will redirect user to your_website/home path.</p>
<p>And this configuration to your nginx.conf file at the end of http block:</p>
<pre><code>        ##
        # Map with redirections from old URLS to new website
        ## 

        map_hash_bucket_size 256;
        map_hash_max_size 65536;

        map $uri $redirect_target {
            default &quot;&quot;;
            include /your_path_to_map/redirects.map;
        }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Both map_hash_bucket_size and map_hash_max_size will depend on size of map file and it's contents, it's up to you. What it does is it creates a hash table and stores it in Nginx memory, so it can be used by it anytime it's needed. Cheers.</p>
